{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>et the manna they ate, etc.</b> It was necessary to say a second time ״et the manna״ and not rely on its mention in the adjacent verse, because there is a difference between the eating of manna that they ate until they came to the inhabited land, and the manna that they ate until they came to the border of the land of Canaan, which is before crossing the Jordan — for that which was until the inhabited land was from the manna kept in the vessels, not that which descended day by day. Therefore when He wished to make mention of the manna that they ate until they came to the land of Canaan, He mentioned it independently, to say that this differs from that. And the intellect will recognize this, for the more time recedes from when it was near the source from which it was generated, the more it is diminished. Further, they said, of blessed memory (Kiddushin 38a): did they then eat it for forty years? Were not thirty days missing, from the 15th of Nisan until the 16th of Iyyar? Rather, they tasted in the cakes the taste of manna." "And I see a fine point to examine: why did the verse change its language? At first it is written <b>they ate et the manna</b> — it placed the mention of eating before the mention of the manna; but when it mentioned the manna a second time it said <b>et the manna they ate</b> — it placed the mention of the manna before the mention of eating. Behold, the verse is precise in its language to tell you that the reckoning of forty years that it counts for the eating of manna is different from what it informs you, that they ate the manna until they came, etc. — apart from the distinction we have already written concerning its daily descent or its being preserved. For the manna that enters into the reckoning of forty years is only in the measure of what they tasted in their eating, and that is why it placed the mention of eating before the mention of the manna: to say that from the perspective of eating it was recognized as being manna, for they tasted in their cakes manna — but it was not all forty years manna in actuality, since for thirty days they ate what they had brought out of Egypt as cakes. And when He wished to set the boundary for the time of eating the manna, He placed its mention first, to say: this is not reckoned as manna from the perspective that they merely tasted so, but according to what it truly was. And examine this well:"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:07.521012253Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}